{"candidates":[{"content":"Artifact 18174034ea0c contradicts the neighbouring position (2f8f85328fae) that commutativity of the consumer's fold operation—not writer-level idempotency—is the necessary property for deterministic replay under at-least-once delivery. The contradiction is substantive, not verbal: 18174034ea0c locates the correctness obligation at the writer (idempotent append ensures the log is identical regardless of retry count), while 2f8f85328fae locates it at the consumer (a commutative, duplicate-suppressing fold tolerates duplicate appends). Both cannot be the uniquely necessary property under a single shared system model. It is refuted if the two properties are shown to be interderivable or equivalent under a common model of at-least-once delivery and append-only replay, or if one is a strict generalization of the other (e.g., writer idempotency is merely a special case of fold commutativity where the fold is identity-with-dedup), collapsing the layer-level disagreement into a reformulation.","evidence_refs":[{"block":"18174034ea0c22d562ec2796e7","quote":"the required property is idempotency because the log's determinism is an outcome property, not merely a harm-avoidance property"},{"block":"2f8f85328fae446c3c422bd4","quote":"it requires commutativity of the fold operation over the event stream"}],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"2f8f85328fae446c3c422bd4286bfcd4df6abfd26babf3005820e939993c4d0b"},{"role":"dependence","target":"3fc33b3603be4326107111e73ee5bfa735e6f92a6da6a64286fc895870602e42"}],"typicality":0.62}],"simulation_proposals":[]}